    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p17-q29-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $$AC^2=\\frac{AB^2+AD^2+2(AB)(AD)\\cos\\theta}{\\sin^2\\theta}.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p17-q29-a-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "AC^2=\\frac{AB^2+AD^2+2(AB)(AD)\\cos\\theta}{\\sin^2\\theta}",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p17-q29-a-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Use the sine rule and equal angles subtended by chord BC to obtain ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "AC=\\frac{BD}{\\sin\\theta}",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "; then apply the cosine rule in ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\triangle ABD",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Angles subtended by chord $BC$ are equal, so $\\angle BDC=\\angle BAC$. The sine rule in $\\triangle BCD$ gives\n$$\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{BD}{\\sin\\theta}&=\\frac{BC}{\\sin\\angle BDC}\\\\\n\\frac{BD}{\\sin\\theta}&=\\frac{BC}{\\sin\\angle BAC}\\\\\n\\frac{BD}{\\sin\\theta}&=AC.\n\\end{align*}$$\nThe last equality uses $\\sin\\angle BAC=\\frac{BC}{AC}$ in right-angled $\\triangle ABC$. Hence\n$$AC^2=\\frac{BD^2}{\\sin^2\\theta}.$$\nOpposite angles of cyclic quadrilateral $ABCD$ are supplementary, so $\\angle BAD=180^\\circ-\\theta$. In $\\triangle ABD$, the cosine rule gives\n$$\\begin{align*}\nBD^2&=AB^2+AD^2-2(AB)(AD)\\cos(180^\\circ-\\theta)\\\\\nBD^2&=AB^2+AD^2+2(AB)(AD)\\cos\\theta.\n\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore,\n$$\\begin{align*}\nAC^2&=\\frac{BD^2}{\\sin^2\\theta}\\\\\nAC^2&=\\frac{AB^2+AD^2+2(AB)(AD)\\cos\\theta}{\\sin^2\\theta}.\n\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 122
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q29-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that $$\\cot^2\\varphi=\\frac{\\cot^2\\alpha+\\cot^2\\beta+2\\cos\\theta\\cot\\alpha\\cot\\beta}{\\sin^2\\theta}.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p17-q29-b-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\cot^2\\varphi=\\frac{\\cot^2\\alpha+\\cot^2\\beta+2\\cos\\theta\\cot\\alpha\\cot\\beta}{\\sin^2\\theta}",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p17-q29-b-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Express AC, AB and AD using the common height AH, substitute into part (a), and cancel ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "AH^2",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Since $AH$ is perpendicular to the circle's plane, $\\triangle HAC$, $\\triangle HAB$ and $\\triangle HAD$ are right-angled at $A$. Thus\n$$\\begin{align*}\nAC&=AH\\cot\\varphi,\\\\\nAB&=AH\\cot\\alpha,\\\\\nAD&=AH\\cot\\beta.\n\\end{align*}$$\nSubstitute into part (a):\n$$AH^2\\cot^2\\varphi=\\frac{AH^2\\cot^2\\alpha+AH^2\\cot^2\\beta+2AH^2\\cos\\theta\\cot\\alpha\\cot\\beta}{\\sin^2\\theta}.$$\nCancel $AH^2$:\n$$\\cot^2\\varphi=\\frac{\\cot^2\\alpha+\\cot^2\\beta+2\\cos\\theta\\cot\\alpha\\cot\\beta}{\\sin^2\\theta}.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 78
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q29-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "If $\\alpha=45^\\circ$, $\\beta=60^\\circ$ and $\\varphi=30^\\circ$, find the acute angle $\\theta$ correct to one decimal place.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p17-q29-c-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\theta=54.7^\\circ",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p17-q29-c-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Substitute into part (b), use ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\sin^2\\theta=1-\\cos^2\\theta",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ", and select the positive cosine because θ is acute.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Using $\\cot45^\\circ=1$, $\\cot60^\\circ=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt3}$ and $\\cot30^\\circ=\\sqrt3$, part (b) gives\n$$\\begin{align*}\n3&=\\frac{1+\\frac13+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt3}\\cos\\theta}{\\sin^2\\theta}\\\\\n3\\sin^2\\theta&=\\frac43+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt3}\\cos\\theta\\\\\n3(1-\\cos^2\\theta)&=\\frac43+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt3}\\cos\\theta\\\\\n9\\cos^2\\theta+2\\sqrt3\\cos\\theta-5&=0\\\\\n(\\sqrt3\\cos\\theta-1)(3\\sqrt3\\cos\\theta+5)&=0.\n\\end{align*}$$\nSince $\\theta$ is acute, $\\cos\\theta>0$, so\n$$\\begin{align*}\n\\cos\\theta&=\\frac1{\\sqrt3}\\\\\n\\theta&=\\cos^{-1}\\!\\left(\\frac1{\\sqrt3}\\right)\\approx54.7^\\circ.\n\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 82
      }
    ]
